    {
      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To determine when the expression \\(\\frac{y+6}{y^2-5y+4}\\) is undefined, we need to identify the values of \\(y\\) that make the denominator zero. The expression is undefined if the denominator \\(y^2 - 5y + 4\\) is zero. Therefore, we need to solve the quadratic equation:\n\n\\[y^2 - 5y + 4 = 0\\]\n\nWe can factorize the quadratic equation as follows:\n\n\\[y^2 - 5y + 4 = (y - 1)(y - 4) = 0\\]\n\nSetting each factor equal to zero gives us the solutions:\n\n\\[y - 1 = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad y - 4 = 0\\]\n\\[y = 1 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad y = 4\\]\n\nThus, the expression \\(\\frac{y+6}{y^2-5y+4}\\) is undefined for \\(y = 1\\) and \\(y = 4\\). The sum of these values is:\n\n\\[1 + 4 = 5\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of all values of \\(y\\) for which the expression is undefined is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
